73. PostgreSQL Procedure

A procedure is different from a function.

Create:

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE hrms.increase_salary(
    employee_id BIGINT,
    increment NUMERIC
)
LANGUAGE plpgsql
AS $$
BEGIN

    UPDATE hrms.employees
    SET salary = salary + increment
    WHERE id = employee_id;

END;
$$;

Call:

CALL hrms.increase_salary(1, 5000);
Key difference

Function:

SELECT hrms.calculate_annual_salary(50000);

Procedure:

CALL hrms.increase_salary(1, 5000);

A function is normally used to return a value/result and can be used inside SQL expressions. A procedure is invoked with CALL and is suited to performing operations.

74. Trigger

Create audit table:

CREATE TABLE hrms.employee_audit (
    id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    employee_id BIGINT,
    old_salary NUMERIC,
    new_salary NUMERIC,
    changed_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);

Create trigger function:

CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION hrms.log_salary_change()
RETURNS TRIGGER
LANGUAGE plpgsql
AS $$
BEGIN

    IF OLD.salary IS DISTINCT FROM NEW.salary THEN

        INSERT INTO hrms.employee_audit
        (
            employee_id,
            old_salary,
            new_salary
        )
        VALUES
        (
            OLD.id,
            OLD.salary,
            NEW.salary
        );

    END IF;

    RETURN NEW;

END;
$$;

Create trigger:

CREATE TRIGGER employee_salary_audit
AFTER UPDATE OF salary
ON hrms.employees
FOR EACH ROW
EXECUTE FUNCTION hrms.log_salary_change();

Now:

UPDATE hrms.employees
SET salary = salary + 5000
WHERE id = 1;

Automatically creates an audit record.

