82. Role-Based Permissions

This is a better approach for enterprise applications.

Create roles:

CREATE ROLE hrms_readonly NOLOGIN;
CREATE ROLE hrms_editor NOLOGIN;

Give permissions:

GRANT SELECT
ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA hrms
TO hrms_readonly;
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE
ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA hrms
TO hrms_editor;

Assign:

GRANT hrms_readonly TO app_user;

or:

GRANT hrms_editor TO hrms_user;

This is much easier to maintain than individually granting permissions to every user.

83. UPSERT – INSERT or UPDATE

One of PostgreSQL's most useful features.

INSERT INTO hrms.employees
(
    email,
    name,
    salary
)
VALUES
(
    'gautam@example.com',
    'Gautam',
    80000
)
ON CONFLICT (email)
DO UPDATE
SET
    name = EXCLUDED.name,
    salary = EXCLUDED.salary;

EXCLUDED represents the value you attempted to insert.

